She Asked Me to Thank CorpsAfrica for Her
I did not expect my first day of one-on-ones to end with someone thanking an organization she'd never met.
But that's exactly how it went: three homes, three completely different lives, and by the end of it, a woman old enough to be my grandmother looked at me and asked me to carry her gratitude back to people she'll probably never see.
Let me start from the beginning.

The Baker Who Remembers What the Village Used to Have
My first stop was the home of Kubwimana Jean and Ingabire Immaculée. Jean runs a small bakery from his home, the kind of quiet, self-taught enterprise that keeps a household going without making much noise about itself.
We talked about his work, his family, and eventually about the wider community.
He told me that the village used to have youth clubs and savings groups. Somewhere along the way, they faded away, and nobody had brought them back.
What struck me was that he did not speak about it simply as a complaint. He spoke about it almost as if he still believed it was possible. He said he sometimes wishes young people would come together again, not just to talk, but to organize themselves and contribute to the community.
I wrote that down.

The One Who Already Has a Plan
The second home belonged to William Kubwimana, and his story moved in a completely different direction.
William sometimes travels to Kigali. He has completed short courses in culinary arts and electronics, two very different skill sets, both of which he is clearly proud of.
He aspires to eventually have his own restaurant.
What interested me most was that his dream was not just an idea. He already has skills and a direction; what remains is to understand what steps and resources could help him move forward.
He also expressed interest in joining a youth group if one existed in the community.
That was the second time in one morning I had heard the same desire.
I made another note.

The Woman Who Turned the Conversation Around on Me
The last visit was with an elderly woman, Beatha Nikuze, and her conversation stayed with me the longest.
We started with her land. The dry season has lasted longer than she hoped, and she is waiting for rain before she can cultivate. For her, that's not simply a weather issue — it affects food, income, and the well-being of the household.
The conversation later moved to another challenge: she had recently struggled to keep up with her Mutuelle de Santé payment.
During our conversation, I shared information about Irembo and Byikorere that could help her understand some of the available services and options.
I thought it was a small thing.

She did not see it that way.
What she said next is the part I keep replaying.
She told me she was amazed, genuinely amazed to sit and have a real conversation with a young person about development and the future of her community.
Then she asked me to pass along her thanks to CorpsAfrica for sending someone young to serve here.
I did not have a response ready for that.
I still do not think I do.

What One Morning Already Taught Me
Three homes. Three different lives.
A baker who remembers community structures that once existed.
A young man with skills and a clear entrepreneurial dream.
An elder woman navigating agricultural uncertainty and household challenges while still thinking about the future of her community.
And somewhere between those three conversations, an early pattern began to emerge.
Twice, independently, people mentioned the absence of youth groups and expressed a desire for young people to come together again.
I do not want to rush to call that the community's biggest need. Three conversations are not enough to make that conclusion.
However, it is a pattern worth listening to.

Therefore, over the coming weeks, I want to explore it further:
Do other young people feel the same way?
What happened to the groups that existed before?
Would young people actually want to rebuild them?
What skills, resources, and relationships already exist within the community?
In addition, if a group is needed, what would the community want that group to become?
For now, I am not looking for answers.
I am listening.
Moreover, perhaps that is the most important thing I can do on my first day of one on one.

